WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.631
Threat hunting is a proactive, analyst-driven search for adversary activity that may not have produced a clear alert.

2
00:00:07.631 --> 00:00:16.407
A hunter starts from a defensive question, a hypothesis, or a known behavior pattern and then looks for evidence in available telemetry.

3
00:00:16.407 --> 00:00:25.031
The work is structured investigation, not casual browsing through logs. It is also not a promise that something malicious is present.

4
00:00:25.031 --> 00:00:32.357
A hunt can confirm suspicious activity, reject a weak idea, reveal telemetry gaps, or produce a better detection.

5
00:00:32.357 --> 00:00:35.409
Threat hunting is different from alert triage.

6
00:00:35.409 --> 00:00:43.804
In triage, the system has already created an alert, and the analyst decides whether it is benign, suspicious, or incident-worthy.

7
00:00:43.804 --> 00:00:51.054
In hunting, the analyst is looking for activity that existing alerts may have missed or did not classify clearly.

8
00:00:51.054 --> 00:00:54.335
Hunting is also different from incident response.

9
00:00:54.335 --> 00:01:01.738
Incident response begins when there is enough evidence to treat the situation as a possible or confirmed incident.

10
00:01:01.738 --> 00:01:10.361
Hunting may feed incident response, but it usually begins earlier and with more uncertainty. Hunting is not vulnerability scanning.

11
00:01:10.361 --> 00:01:19.748
A scanner asks whether assets have known weaknesses. A hunt asks whether behavior in the environment suggests suspicious or adversary activity.

12
00:01:19.748 --> 00:01:22.419
Hunting is not compliance review either.

13
00:01:22.419 --> 00:01:31.424
Compliance work may check whether required controls exist, while hunting tests operational visibility and asks what the telemetry shows.

14
00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:37.758
A good security program can include all of these activities, but they answer different questions.

15
00:01:37.758 --> 00:01:45.465
In a SOC, hunting fits between detection engineering, alert operations, threat intelligence, and incident response.

16
00:01:45.465 --> 00:01:52.257
Hunters use telemetry from endpoints, identity systems, DNS, cloud logs, proxy logs, and other sources.

17
00:01:52.257 --> 00:02:01.491
They may use ATT&CK-style behavior mapping, recent incident lessons, business context, or known environmental risks to decide where to look.

18
00:02:01.491 --> 00:02:07.520
When a hunt produces a useful pattern, detection engineers can turn it into repeatable logic.

19
00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:16.372
When a hunt finds suspicious evidence, incident responders can take over with an escalation process. The defensive discipline matters.

20
00:02:16.372 --> 00:02:21.943
A hunt should have authorization, scope, notes, and evidence handling expectations.

21
00:02:21.943 --> 00:02:30.719
It should define the environment, time window, data sources, assumptions, and decision criteria. Hunters should avoid overclaiming.

22
00:02:30.719 --> 00:02:37.206
Unusual is not automatically malicious, and absence of evidence is not proof that nothing happened.

23
00:02:37.206 --> 00:02:47.661
A practical hunt produces a documented outcome: evidence found, evidence not found, a refined hypothesis, a telemetry gap, a tuned alert, or a new detection idea.

24
00:02:47.661 --> 00:02:53.690
That is what makes hunting valuable as an engineering practice rather than random searching.

25
00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:57.887
A useful way to evaluate a hunt is to ask what decision it supports.

26
00:02:57.887 --> 00:03:06.434
The decision might be to escalate, close the hypothesis, improve a detection, request better logging, or schedule a follow-up hunt.

27
00:03:06.434 --> 00:03:11.242
That decision focus keeps the work from becoming open-ended exploration.

28
00:03:11.242 --> 00:03:18.339
It also helps managers and other analysts understand why the hunt was worth doing even if no incident was found.
